  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 PM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nPRECIPITATION CHANCES:\n\nLATE TONIGHT--WEDNESDAY MORNING...A plume of rich mid-level moisture \nwithin a zone of modest 800-600mb warm advection may support a few \nsprinkles and/or showers late tonight into Wednesday morning, mainly \nnorthwest of a Lyons to Salina line. Could see a few lightning \nstrikes given steep lapse rates above the mid-level moist plume, but \nsevere weather or heavy rainfall is not expected.\n\nLATE WEDNESDAY NIGHT--SATURDAY MORNING...An ejecting upper low \ncurrently spinning off the coast of the Baja peninsula will be the \nfocus for off-and-on chances for showers/thunderstorms late \nWednesday night through Saturday morning, as it gradually ejects \nnortheastward. Thinking chances will be highest mainly northwest of \nthe KS Turnpike through Friday evening, with chances expanding \neastward Friday night--Saturday morning as the upper wave finally \nmoves through the area. Limited instability, poor mid-level lapse \nrates, and modest-at-best deep layer shear should prevent severe \nweather, although could see a few strong storms Friday late \nafternoon-evening when instability and shear increase a bit. \nAdditionally, anomalous precipitable waters may support locally \nheavy rainfall at times, especially Thursday evening through early \nSaturday.\n\nEARLY--MID NEXT WEEK...Model consensus supports increasing chances \nfor showers/thunderstorms once again sometime early to mid next \nweek, as a potent upper trough traverses Mid-America. The potential \nfor stout forcing amidst increasing buoyancy and deep layer shear \nmay support severe weather along with heavy rainfall. There is still \nquite a bit of uncertainty surrounding this period (namely upper \ntrough timing, magnitude, placement), stay tuned.\n\nTEMPERATURES:\n\nPer model consensus, above average heights/thickness will support \nmostly above average temperatures through about mid next week, with \nforecast highs in the 80s to near 90 degrees, likely warmest \nSaturday through Monday. Temperatures should cool some sometime mid-\nlate next week in wake of a stout upper trough, but ensemble data \nonly supports a modest cool down at best.",
